      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Categorising incidents (VPN, database, printer, network) gives a strong grouping axis for identifying operational burdens. Values map cleanly to short_description text across samples."
    },
    {
      "name": "failure_symptom",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.88,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Symptom typing (connectivity_failure, access_denied, outage) is a genuine analytical axis for burden analysis. Well-grounded in descriptions; 'Unknown' correctly used for vague 'Printing issues'."
    },
    {
      "name": "affected_resource_scope",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.78,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Server/client/network scope is useful for locating burden. Mostly reasonable inference from category, though some assignments (e.g., database -> server_side, printer -> client_side) are heuristic rather than explicit in short text."
    },
    {
      "name": "incident_blast_radius",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.82,
      "source_groundedness": 0.62,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Scope of impact is a valuable burden axis. However short descriptions like 'Cannot connect to office WiFi' rarely state whether one user or the whole system is affected; 'individual_user' vs 'system_wide' is often an unwarranted specific inference."
    },
    {
      "name": "self_resolved",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.55,
      "source_groundedness": 0.35,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Could inform burden, but the value is not derivable: incidents show a closed_by person (e.g., 'Fred Luddy'), which contradicts self-resolution, yet self_resolved=True is asserted. Not grounded in available fields."
    },
    {
      "name": "recurrent_caller",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.6,
      "source_groundedness": 0.45,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Repeat callers are relevant to burden, but derivation requires caller_id aggregation not shown in samples; a 400/100 skew with True on every sample suggests a possibly unsupported blanket assignment. Weakly grounded."
    },
    {
      "name": "off_hours_incident",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Directly derivable from opened_at timestamps (e.g., 18:14, 22:35 -> True; 8:50, 7:34 -> False checks out). Useful for staffing/burden analysis."
    },
    {
      "name": "recurrence_signal",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.4,
      "source_groundedness": 0.4,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Largely redundant with recurrent_caller in intent, and recurrence is not evidenced in single-row short descriptions. Poorly grounded and adds limited new leverage."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.72,
  "source_groundedness": 0.63,
  "rationale": "The category/symptom/off_hours columns are strong: genuine analytical axes for characterising operational burdens and well-grounded in short_description and opened_at. Scope and blast_radius are useful but rest on heuristic inferences the terse text does not fully support. The behavioural flags (self_resolved, recurrent_caller, recurrence_signal) are the weakest: self_resolved contradicts the present closed_by field, and recurrence signals require cross-row aggregation not evidenced, yielding several unsupported confident values. Overall the set offers real analytical leverage but with notable groundedness gaps in the flag columns."
}
